# Level 1 → 2 — Reading a Dash-Named File

## Level Goal
Read the password stored in a file named `-` in the home directory.

## Description
A file named `-` is a classic Linux gotcha — a leading dash causes the shell to interpret the name as a command-line flag rather than a filename. Understanding how the shell parses arguments is a critical skill.

## Approach
After logging in as `bandit1`, I confirmed the file existed:

```bash
ls
```

My first instinct was to try `cat -`, but this caused the terminal to hang, waiting for standard input (stdin) instead of reading the file. The fix was to use a relative path prefix:

```bash
cat ./-
```

This worked immediately and returned the password.

> Password obtained. [Not shared per OverTheWire rules]

## Key Takeaway
`./` tells the shell "this is a path in the current directory," not a flag. This applies to any tool — for example, `rm ./-` to delete such a file.
